Certainly! When you want to make multiple API calls and wait for all of them to complete, you can use `Promise.all`. This method takes an array of promises and returns a single promise that resolves to an array of the results of the input promises. If any of the promises are rejected, the returned promise will be rejected with the reason of the first rejected promise.

Here's a basic example to illustrate how you can use `Promise.all` with a list of API calls:

```javascript
// Example API calls
const apiCalls = [
  fetch('https://api.example.com/data1'),
  fetch('https://api.example.com/data2'),
  fetch('https://api.example.com/data3')
];

// Use Promise.all to wait for all API calls to complete
Promise.all(apiCalls)
  .then(responses => {
    // responses is an array of Response objects
    return Promise.all(responses.map(response => response.json()));
  })
  .then(data => {
    // data is an array of the JSON data from each API call
    console.log(data);
  })
  .catch(error => {
    // Handle any errors that occurred during the API calls
    console.error('Error:', error);
  });
```

### Explanation:

1. **API Calls**: We create an array of `fetch` calls to make multiple API requests.
2. **Promise.all**: We use `Promise.all` to wait for all the fetch requests to complete. This returns a promise that resolves to an array of the responses from the fetch calls.
3. **Map and JSON Parsing**: We map over the array of responses and use `response.json()` to parse the JSON data from each response.
4. **Second Promise.all**: We use another `Promise.all` to wait for all the JSON parsing to complete.
5. **Success Handling**: If all the API calls and JSON parsing are successful, we log the data.
6. **Error Handling**: If any of the API calls fail, the promise will be rejected, and the error will be caught and logged.

### Handling Different Data Types:

If the API responses are not all JSON, you can modify the code accordingly. For example, if some responses are text and some are JSON, you might need to handle them differently:

```javascript
const apiCalls = [
  fetch('https://api.example.com/data1'),
  fetch('https://api.example.com/data2').then(response => response.text()),
  fetch('https://api.example.com/data3').then(response => response.json())
];

Promise.all(apiCalls)
  .then(responses => {
    return Promise.all(responses.map(response => {
      if (response.headers.get('content-type')?.includes('application/json')) {
        return response.json();
      } else {
        return response.text();
      }
    }));
  })
  .then(data => {
    console.log(data);
  })
  .catch(error => {
    console.error('Error:', error);
  });
```

In this example, we check the `Content-Type` header to determine how to handle the response.

This should give you a good starting point for making multiple API calls and handling their results in JavaScript using `Promise.all`.
